---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do hosts trust signed models in Traverse?"
  description="Discover → execute → trace on one shared runtime.wasm. Host-owned trust roots gate register_package. v0.15.0 requires model_usage and runs exact-ref on every published host."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-hosts-trust-signed-models.html"
  category="Runtime and Architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the host owns the trust roots. Packages enter only through <code>register_package</code> / <code>registerPackage</code> after signature and digest checks against pins the app already declared. A naked URL is never identity.</p>

  <h2>Signed packages</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.14.0">Traverse v0.14.0</a> landed signed Spec 138: schema <code>2.0.0</code> manifests and a detached Ed25519 <code>model.sig.json</code> over the exact <code>model.manifest.json</code> bytes. <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.15.0">v0.15.0</a> still verifies those packages, plus schema <code>2.1.0</code> and <code>2.2.0</code>. App manifests pin those bytes (SHA-256), plus <code>target</code>, expected <code>rights</code>, and an optional signer <code>key_id</code>. Historical embedder notes: <a href="/blog/traverse-0-14-0-what-changed-for-embedders.html">what changed for embedders in v0.14.0</a>.</p>

  <h2>Host-owned trust roots</h2>
  <p>Trust lists live on the host (<code>TrustedModelKeys</code> on Rust, <code>trustedPublicKeysHex</code> on web and Swift <code>ExactModelHost</code>). Applications cannot inject keys. Registration verifies the signature against a trusted key, the digest against exactly one pin, and the manifest schema (unknown fields fail closed), rights, target, and limits. Every execute re-hashes cached bytes. v0.15.0 also requires <code>model_usage</code>. Without it, the call fails closed with <code>usage_undeclared</code>.</p>

  <h2>Test-only key vs production</h2>
  <p>The first trained package <code>digits-mlp-1.0.0</code> is signed with a <strong>test-only</strong> key so CI and demos can exercise the path. v0.15.0 ships key-generation tooling and an opt-in signing workflow. Custody, distribution, rotation, and revocation are still <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1567">#1567</a>. Do not treat the test key as a production trust root.</p>

  <h2>Host honesty</h2>
  <p>Discover → execute → trace, on one shared <code>runtime.wasm</code>. Published exact-ref execute in v0.15.0 is native Rust, the web embedder, Swift <code>ExactModelHost</code> on <code>swift-host-v0.15.0</code>, and Kotlin and .NET <code>ExactModelHost</code> in the 0.15.0 Maven and NuGet packages. Third-party ONNX models can be packaged, signed, and verified with <code>traverse-cli model</code>. Check <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a>. Deeper loop: <a href="/questions/what-is-exact-ref-model-execution.html">What is exact-ref model execution?</a></p>
</QuestionLayout>
